  "id": "high-plank-crossbody-drag",
  "name": "Plank Drag",
  "definition": "An alternating high-plank anti-rotation drill. The athlete holds a high plank on both palms with the elbows straight, a single weight rests on the floor just behind one hand, the opposite hand drags it along the floor beneath the torso to the other side, and the athlete re-establishes two-palm support before the other arm drags it back. The NSCA bridge pull-through defines the high-plank start, the weight placement behind one hand, the alternating pull-through, and the cue to keep the shoulders and hips parallel to the floor without twisting. It names a sandbag, filled water bottle, pack, or other equipment and gives dumbbell instructions, so any single draggable weight belongs to this contract. Keeping the weight in floor contact is a catalog narrowing of that standard. The training-defining task is resisting spinal extension and rotation while support changes; the arm drag is not assigned an unsupported prime joint action. Can et al. measured serratus-anterior, triceps, and infraspinatus activity in four-point high planks and reduced-support plank tasks; the serratus, external-rotator, and triceps stabilizer roles are consistent with that supporting evidence, not exact-fixture measurements. The wrist, hand, and lower-body stabilizer roles are anatomy-and-mechanics assignments, not measured findings. Plank Drag logs the mass of the single dragged weight as an owner-selected logging convention: that mass is the comparable load within this exercise only. Floor surface, implement shape, and drag distance still change the demand, so the value is not a measured resistance or joint force. Renegade rows, multiple-implement transfers, forearm planks, push-ups, carries, and same-side pulls remain outside this family.",
